From 69dd008c298fca2a3888876ac68b2d31c18c194b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Thu, 9 Jul 2026 15:18:29 +0930
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] irqchip: add aspeed,ast2050-vic driver for the G3
 (AST2050/AST1100) VIC
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The AST2050 (SoC generation 3) interrupt controller is a compact VIC at 0x1e6c0000
with a non-interleaved 32-source register layout (datasheet §16): VIC00 STATUS,
VIC10 ENABLE, VIC14 ENABLE_CLR, VIC24 SENSE (1=level), VIC28 DUAL_EDGE, VIC2C EVENT
(1=high/rising), VIC38 EDGE_CLEAR. This is functionally the same VIC as the AST2400
but the AST2400+ moved it to an interleaved map at 0x1e6c0080 that irq-aspeed-vic.c
drives -- its register writes miss the G3 entirely, so no interrupt is ever enabled,
the timer clockevent is dead, hrtimers hang, and boot stalls at local_irq_enable().

irq-aspeed-g3-vic.c programs sense/event/dual per datasheet Table 36 (timers 16-18 and
WDT 27 = rising-edge, RTC 22-26 = both-edge, peripherals = high-level) since the P2A
reset-boot path has no firmware to do it, and ACKs edge sources via VIC38. Bind with a
DT node: compatible="aspeed,ast2050-vic", reg=<0x1e6c0000 0x1000>.

Verified on real AST2050 hardware: clockevent fires at ~1kHz, eth0 links up with real
interrupts, kernel IP-config completes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WwQmDxXnjQ3ZinKocvVHfa
